## 4. The UI

Landscape, dark, laid out like a car head unit rather than a phone app. Both pieces of
chrome **float over** the content: a status strip at the top (clock, date, outside temp,
link chip, radio glyphs, profile mark) and a dock pill at the bottom. Floating rather than
stacked is what lets the map and the album art run edge to edge behind them.

It implements the rendered mockups in [branding/mockups/png/](branding/mockups/png/) — open
`02-dashboard.png` beside the app and they should match. `mockups/warivo.css` and
`ui/theme/Theme.kt` carry the same palette and geometry and **must be changed together**.

- **Home** — the landing panel: a greeting, a non-interactive map card with a search
  pill and saved-place shortcuts, and a right column of vehicle state (battery ring,
  parked/riding, range, odo/trip) and now-playing. Answers "can I go, and where" without
  the full cluster.
- **Boot → PIN → Home** — the splash hands off to an Ola-style 4-digit PIN pad
  (`ui/PinLockScreen.kt`), not straight to the dashboard. The PIN is the *head unit's*
  ignition: it keeps a stranger out of the dashboard, trip log and tracking settings, but
  the scooter still rides, because the node drives nothing. No fingerprint key —
  `BiometricPrompt` needs a `FragmentActivity` host and the sensor is usually behind the
  cradle. The default-PIN hint shows only until the PIN is changed, so it cannot become a
  password printed on the dashboard.
- **About** — opens from Settings. Every value is read from the device, not hardcoded,
  because the only time anyone opens About is when something is wrong. Includes a plain
  **Privacy & data** page listing exactly what the uplink sends, which matters now that it
  can send a position every few seconds.
- **Dock** — brand mark, then the six panels; on the right, the controls the *phone*
  actually owns: proximity beep, media volume, kiosk lock. The mockups draw car controls
  there, but Warivo OS displays scooter state and must never command the scooter.
- **Drive** — three columns. Speed ring on the left with the ride-mode segment beneath it
  and a telltale strip; battery ring plus estimated range with a segmented meter in the
  middle; four stat tiles down the right. Speed gets the biggest ring because it is the
  only value read while moving; the tiles are what you check at a stop.
- **Map** — full-bleed MapLibre with a floating destination pill, a recentre button, a
  speed bubble and a position card.
- **Music** — artwork, title, progress and five transport controls on the left; output and
  queue cards on the right, so changing track never hides what is playing.
- **Search** — the Google wordmark, a tall pill field, shortcut chips and recent searches;
  searching swaps in a Google-only WebView.
- **Settings** — four quick toggles (WiFi, Bluetooth, GPS, kiosk) over two detail cards.

There is also a **launch splash** (ring, monogram, wordmark, progress) drawn natively in
Compose rather than loading `boot-animation.html` in a WebView — a WebView means starting
a browser engine on the critical path of the thing whose job is to look instant. It is
*not* the Android boot animation, which needs `/system/media/bootanimation.zip` and
therefore root or the ROM.

Panels do **not** swipe. The map and the search WebView both consume horizontal drags, so
a pager would fight them on exactly the two panels where it matters. The dock shrinks
below 900dp of width: the mockup is a 1600dp head-unit screen, and six tiles plus four
controls at that size would push the controls off a 730dp phone.

Deliberate departures from the mockups, all because the underlying capability is missing
rather than for convenience: the map's **turn-by-turn and ETA cards** are absent (no
routing engine, no destination); **album art** is a gradient tile (real artwork needs an
image loader); the **ride-mode segment is read-only** (it reflects the scooter's gear
switch, and making it tappable would imply the app can change gear); and **shuffle/repeat
are dimmed** (`MediaPlayer` has no queue model).

## 5. Things that will bite you

Learned while writing this; all are commented at the relevant line.

- **BLE MTU is not optional.** A telemetry frame is ~140 bytes, but the default BLE MTU of
  23 caps a notification at 20. Without `requestMtu(247)` *before* service discovery,
  every frame arrives truncated and unparseable — which looks exactly like broken
  firmware. This is the single most likely cause of "the node sends nothing".
- **One GATT operation at a time.** Android silently drops a write issued while another is
  outstanding, so the CCCD subscribe and all writes go through a queue. Without it, 1 Hz
  GPS writes randomly eat the subscribe and telemetry never starts.
- **Locale breaks JSON.** GPS writes are formatted with `Locale.US`; on a phone set to a
  comma-decimal locale, the default would hand the firmware `{"lat":26,846700}`.
- **Trip distance comes from the odometer delta**, not from integrating speed, so a dropped
  BLE link costs no distance. A negative delta is read as a node reboot, not as travel.
- **A connected link is not a live link.** GATT can stay nominally connected while
  notifications dry up — the node browns out, resets, or drifts out of range without a
  clean disconnect. The client runs a 2.5 s watchdog and exposes `stale`; the dashboard
  dims and says so. Without it a frozen speedometer reading 40 km/h looks exactly like a
  working one, which is worse than showing nothing.
- **`gear: 0` does not mean first gear.** The node now sends `gear` on every frame, using
  0 for "no switch fitted". The parser maps 0 to null, because the dashboard treats a
  present gear as a wired gear switch and would otherwise light the ride-mode segment as
  "Eco" on a scooter that has no switch at all.
- **Common ground or nothing.** ESP32 ground must tie to battery negative, or every ADC
  reading is meaningless.
- **Never drive a scooter line.** The taps in `audit.md` are read-only. Injecting voltage
  onto a throttle signal could command unintended acceleration.

