## 6. The ROM (Path B)

**Decided: AOSP via LineageOS, shipped first as a Treble GSI.** A ROM *is* a Linux kernel
plus Android's userspace, and [os/](os/) is laid out that way: `kernel/` holds the config
fragment, `device/warivo/generic_arm64/` the board and product definition, `vendor/warivo/`
the overlays and the launcher prebuilt, and `build/` the scripts that sync a tree, graft
Warivo in and build it.

Not a *bare* Linux userspace, though — Reasoning, prerequisites and build steps are in
[docs/ROM_BUILD.md](docs/ROM_BUILD.md); the device-independent product config is scaffolded
in [os/](os/).

everything the launcher does — Device Owner, Lock Task, BLE, A2DP, `MediaStore`,
`WebView`, Compose — *is* the Android framework. Replacing Android's userspace means
discarding the launcher and reimplementing all of it against BlueZ, PipeWire and an
embedded browser engine: years of work for a scooter dashboard, and a rider would never
notice the difference. A Treble GSI, meanwhile, skips device trees
and vendor blobs entirely, and the things GSIs usually break (camera, fingerprint, VoLTE)
are things a dashboard does not use.

`os/build/build-rom.sh` is the entry point and refuses to start rather than failing hours
in. Two hard stops remain: **a Linux build host** (AOSP does not build on macOS) with
~400 GB free, and **the phone's model, codename and unlock status** for anything beyond a
GSI. Path A's
kiosk already delivers ~95% of the experience — the ROM adds a boot animation, no setup
wizard, no GApps and radios-on-at-first-boot.

## 7. Fleet tracking

Position uplink, alerts, remote lock and the owner app. The contract is
[docs/FLEET.md](docs/FLEET.md); the head unit side is `launcher/.../fleet/`, the owner app
is [companion/](companion/).

The head unit records a position every 5–30 s and sends telemetry up and config and
commands down **in one request per interval** — a scooter moves through patchy coverage, so
polling separately for commands would double the traffic to learn nothing most of the time.
Samples spool to disk (about 5½ hours at 10 s), so a ride through a dead zone appears on the
owner's map afterwards instead of vanishing.

Alerts — speed, battery, zone entry/exit, power-on — are evaluated **on the device**, so
they still fire with no signal and upload later with their original timestamp. All are
edge-triggered with hysteresis; a level-triggered speed alert hovering at the threshold
would emit an event every sample.

Two things that shape the design and are worth reading in full in the doc:

- **Remote lock cannot immobilise the scooter.** The node reads sensors and drives nothing.
  "Lock" replaces the head unit's display with the owner's message and keeps it reporting.
  Cutting power to a moving vehicle is a crash, so the command vocabulary deliberately has
  no verb for it.
- **Continuous tracking is visibly on.** A dot in the status bar in every state including
  offline, a row in Settings, and off entirely until an endpoint is configured. If the
  rider is not the owner, telling them is your job — the app is built so you can.

## 8. Recently added

- **Home panel** (`ui/HomePanel.kt`) — greeting, map card, saved places, vehicle and
  now-playing cards.
- **Launch splash** (`ui/BootSplash.kt`).
- **Speed alert** (`alert/SpeedChime.kt`) — the Settings mockup promised it, so it is real
  now. Edge-triggered with a 3 km/h hysteresis band: a chime that repeats while you are
  over the limit is noise you learn to ignore, and hovering exactly on the threshold would
  otherwise chime continuously.
- **Saved places** — Home/Work, set from the current GPS fix in Settings, shown on Home as
  straight-line distance. Not an ETA: there is no routing engine, so a time would be
  invented. No geocoder either, which is why they are set from "here" rather than typed.
- **Shared map surface** (`ui/MapSurface.kt`) — Home and Map both use it. Safe because
  panels are swapped with `when`, so only one GL surface exists at a time.

### And since that

- **Obstacle reading is visible again** — a regression from the three-column rework: the
  IR distance was still parsed and still drove the proximity beep, but nothing on screen
  showed it, so the only way to know why the phone had beeped was to guess. It is now a
  bar in the aux card plus a red chip when inside the trigger distance.
- **Throttle** — `thr` renders as a meter in the same card.
- **Real album art** — `MusicPlayer.artworkFor` uses `ContentResolver.loadThumbnail`
  (API 29), which decodes and downscales for us, so covers cost no image-loading
  dependency. Decoded on the IO dispatcher keyed to the track, because a dropped frame on
  a moving vehicle is worse than a late cover. Android 9 and art-less files keep the
  brand gradient, which is why it has to look deliberate.
- **`tools/check_kotlin.py`** — the static check that stands in for a compiler. It now
  also validates Compose extension imports, which is how it earns its place: that class
  of mistake was previously invisible to it.

### And since that

- **boot → PIN → home** (`ui/PinLockScreen.kt`), the pulsing glowing mark
  (`GlowingMark`), the "Built by Enjoys" boot credit, and **About**
  (`ui/AboutScreen.kt`) with hand-maintained licences and a privacy page.
- The dock's lock button now locks the *screen* behind the PIN. Kiosk provisioning and the
  escape hatch stay in Settings — a dock button that could hand a stranger the whole phone
  does not belong next to the volume keys.
- Fixed `os/build/build-bootanimation.sh`, which defaulted to **portrait** 1080×1920 while
  `branding/bootanimation/desc.txt` says landscape 1920×1080. Rendering portrait frames
  into a landscape descriptor gives an animation that plays letterboxed and sideways.

### Also added since

- **Ride log** (`trip/RideHistory.kt`) — the last 20 completed rides, with distance,
  average, max, duration and consumption, listed in Settings. Rides are segmented out of
  the telemetry stream by the wheel sensor: a ride starts when the wheel first turns and
  ends after three minutes of not turning, so a traffic light stays one ride but a parked
  scooter closes it. Nothing to start or stop.
- **Link staleness** — `WarivoNodeClient.stale`, surfaced as an amber status chip and a
  dimmed dashboard.
- **The node's new fields** — the firmware now reports `glim` (the gear's speed cap),
  `avg`, `whkm`, `mil` and `cyc`. The dashboard shows the cap under the ride-mode segment
  and prefers the node's trip average over the phone's, because the node keeps counting
  through a BLE dropout where the phone-side figure silently loses that stretch. Battery
  health (cycles, Wh/km, km per charge) is a Settings row.

## 9. Open questions

- **Licence** — nothing chosen yet. Needed before anyone else can use this.
- **Offline maps** — ship as-is with MapLibre's opportunistic disk cache, or add
  `OfflineManager` to pre-download a region? Worth knowing before starting: MapLibre's
  offline region definition wants a style **URL**, and ours is inline JSON, so the style
  would have to be written to a file and referenced as `file://`. The observer callback
  names also still carry Mapbox-era spellings that differ between MapLibre versions —
  check them against the version actually resolved rather than trusting a snippet.
- **Units** — the Settings mockup shows a Metric/Imperial switch. Not built: it means
  routing every readout through a formatter, and for an Indian scooter metric is the only
  case that matters. Worth doing only if someone actually wants miles.
- **OSM tiles** — `tile.openstreetmap.org` is fine for one personal phone, but its usage
  policy forbids heavy use. A second scooter means bringing your own tile source or a
  MapTiler key.
- **Boot animation** — art exists in `branding/`; packaging it as `bootanimation.zip` needs
  root on stock Android, so it realistically waits for Path B.
- **Multimeter readings** — the one thing blocking the throttle/gear/light taps.
  [audit.md](audit.md) §4 is the checklist.
